:           ds/IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/file_output_stream.cljc:111:           ds/IDaoStreamReader\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:155:  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:164:  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:180:  stream/IDaoStreamClosable\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/file_input_stream.cljc:26:          ds/IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/file_input_stream.cljc:33:          ds/IDaoStreamReader\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/file_input_stream.cljc:104:     ds/IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/file_input_stream.cljc:111:     ds/IDaoStreamReader\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:42:  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:53:  stream/IDaoStreamReader\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:110:  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:135:  stream/IDaoStreamClosable\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:211:     ds/IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:220:     ds/IDaoStreamReader\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:275:          ds/IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:284:          ds/IDaoStreamReader\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:340:     ds/IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:349:     ds/IDaoStreamReader\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:89:          ;; IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:91:          ds/IDaoStreamWriter\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:109:          ;; IDaoStreamReader\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:111:          ds/IDaoStreamReader\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/telemetry.cljc:58:(defn type-tag\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:384:(defn- flush-telemetry\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:440:                                                    flush-telemetry)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/telemetry.cljc:115:(defn type-tag\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/telemetry.cljc:124:        (satisfies? ds/IDaoStreamReader value) :dao-stream\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/telemetry.cljc:166:      (satisfies? ds/IDaoStreamReader value)\n   140\t;; =============================================================================\n   141\t;; Handle Protocols (Five Handle Operations)\n   142\t;; =============================================================================\n   143\t\n   144\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamDescriptor\n   145\t  \"Universal reachability and identity projection. Belongs to no surface:\n   146\t   implemented by every handle regardless of declared reader/writer/closable surfaces.\"\n   147\t\n   148\t  (descriptor\n   149\t    [handle]\n   150\t    \"Returns descriptor outcome map:\n   151\t     {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   152\t      :dao.stream/descriptor <portable-descriptor>\n   153\t      :dao.stream/identity <logical-stream-identity>}\"))\n   154\t\n   155\t\n   156\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamReader\n   157\t  \"Reader surface: positioned, non-destructive observation via immutable cursors.\"\n   158\t\n   159\t  (cursor\n   160\t    [handle anchor]\n   161\t    \"Mint an immutable cursor from anchor (:dao.stream/oldest, :dao.stream/newest,\n   162\t     or transport-specific anchor).\n   163\t     Returns outcome map with :dao.stream/cursor on :dao.stream/ok.\")\n   164\t\n   165\t  (next\n   166\t    [handle cursor]\n   167\t    \"Read the value at cursor position. Total and non-blocking.\n   168\t     Returns read outcome map.\"))\n   169\t\n   170\t\n   171\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamWriter\n   172\t  \"Writer surface: append values to the sequence this handle's writer surface is on.\"\n   173\t\n   174\t  (append!\n   175\t    [handle val]\n   176\t    \"Append val to the sequence. Total and non-blocking.\n   177\t     Returns write outcome map.\"))\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\t(defprotocol IDaoStreamClosable\n   181\t  \"Closable surface: close what this handle is on (logical stream or attachment).\n   182\t   Idempotent.\"\n   183\t\n   184\t  (close!\n   185\t    [handle]\n   186\t    \"Closes what this handle is on. Irrevocable.\n   187\t     Returns outcome map {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}.\"))\n   188\t\n   189\t\n   190\t;; =============================================================================\n   191\t;; Surface Inspection & Gating\n   192\t;; =============================================================================\n   193\t\n   194\t(defn reader?\n   195\t  \"True if handle implements the reader surface (IDaoStreamReader).\"\n   196\t  [handle]\n   197\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamReader handle))\n   198\t\n   199\t\n   200\t(defn writer?\n   201\t  \"True if handle implements the writer surface (IDaoStreamWriter).\"\n   202\t  [handle]\n   203\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamWriter handle))\n   204\t\n   205\t\n   206\t(defn closable?\n   207\t  \"True if handle implements the closable surface (IDaoStreamClosable).\"\n   208\t  [handle]\n   209\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamClosable handle))\n   210\t\n   211\t\n   212\t(defn descriptor?\n   213\t  \"True if handle implements IDaoStreamDescriptor.\"\n   214\t  [handle]\n   215\t  (satisfies? IDaoStreamDescriptor handle))\n   216\t\n   217\t\n   218\t(defn declared-surfaces\n   219\t  \"Returns set of public surfaces implemented by handle:\n   220\t   subset of #{:reader :writer :closable}.\"\n   221\t  [handle]\n   222\t  (cond-> #{}\n   223\t    (reader? handle) (conj :reader)\n   224\t    (writer? handle) (conj :writer)\n   225\t    (closable? handle) (conj :closable)))\n   226\t\n   227\t\n   228\t;; =============================================================================\n   229\t;; Result Convention & Envelope Validation\n   230\t;; =============================================================================\n   365\t      ;; encoded request, with the same id, is retried on a later step.\n   366\t      :yin.repl.adapter/pending-request\n   367\t      (assoc state :retrying line)\n   368\t\n   369\t      (-> state\n   370\t          (assoc :retrying nil)\n   371\t          (publish :yin.repl.driver/notice\n   372\t                   (str \";; request not sent: \"\n   373\t                        (name (:yin.repl.adapter/outcome result))))))))\n   374\t\n   375\t\n   376\t(defn- handle-line\n   377\t  [state line]\n   378\t  (let [command (adapter/local-command line)]\n   379\t    (cond\n   380\t      ;; A shell that has quit evaluates nothing more.  Hosts append an\n   381\t      ;; end-of-input `(quit)` that a typed `(quit)` has already answered.\n   382\t      (not (:running? state)) state\n   383\t\n   384\t      (not (string? line))\n   385\t      (publish state :yin.repl.driver/diagnostic\n   386\t               (str \";; ignored non-string input: \" (pr-str line)))\n   387\t\n   388\t      (str/blank? line) state\n   389\t\n   390\t      ;; Local control commands bypass the remote queue, so a stuck or slow\n145:(defn- host-stream?\n158:               (if (host-stream? v)\n41:3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n42:   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n43:4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n44:   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n48:Creation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\n49:identity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n58:| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n59:| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n85:- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n87:  Projecting that identity never fails.\n91:descriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n104:  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n204:  new logical stream. `descriptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n205:  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n206:  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n207:  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n208:  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n209:  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n211:- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n212:  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n214:- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only\n232:`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n236:registry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n237:somewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n253:The **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object\n254:reference. It is reachability data, not the logical-stream identity itself.\n259:- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n260:  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n262:  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n263:  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n267:  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n271:  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n276:  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n282:  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n290:For the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`\n2:name: Problem domain vocabulary for naming\n3:description: Vocabulary from biology, economics, physics, and philosophy to guide naming and enable stigmergic emergence\n11:The goal is for **stigmergy to emerge from sharing of data**: agents coordinate indirectly through environmental modification (shared streams), not through top-down commands or explicit messaging.\n13:Think ant colonies: individual ants don't know the colony's plan, but pheromone trails on the ground coordinate behavior. That's stigmergic coordination emerging from simple data sharing.\n43:- **Value**: Worth in exchange. How useful data is to an interpreter.\n44:- **Arbitrage**: Exploiting differences in value across contexts. Reinterpreting data for new purposes.\n45:- **Market**: A place where exchange happens. Streams as markets for data.\n65:- **Interference**: Waves meeting and combining. Agents coordinating through shared data.\n78:- **Interpretation**: Extracting meaning from data. Same data, different observers, different truths.\n89:- **Choose names that describe the function from the problem perspective**: What role does this play in the ecosystem?\n90:- **Use metaphors that illuminate**: A name that makes the purpose obvious without documentation.\n91:- **Consider the namespace as a niche**: What is this module's ecological role?\n96:- **Use generic names**: Avoid `data`, `info`, `thing`, `state`\n97:- **Use implementation detail as the name**: The name should not reveal how, only why and what\n98:- **Hide domain intent**: The name should make the problem domain visible\n118:Remember: the goal is for coordination to emerge from **shared data**, not from centralized commands.\n124:- **Feedback loops**: Effects become visible as new data, feeding back into decisions\n126:When naming, ask: **\"Does this name make it obvious how this module contributes to stigmergic coordination?\"**\n128:If the name is opaque or generic, it probably doesn't.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Two issues are emerging: the lazy-sequence rule promises more than limiting traversal can guarantee, and the stream row confuses identity with the full descriptor. The driver citation also needs correction: line 386 handles non-string input, so a long string reaches it only inside another value. I’m checking the remaining claimed duplicates and portability assumptions.\n"}}
