616:  ([^String s start] (. s (substring start)))\n3617:  ([^String s start end] (. s (substring start end))))\n     1\tCitations check out. The deliverable is written and left unstaged.\n     2\t\n     3\t**Conclusion: a shared abstraction is warranted, but smaller than the brief's framing.** The design is in `docs/design/dao.summary.md`, marked as a proposal, not reviewed or approved.\n     4\t\n     5\t**What I found.** The pattern recurs across the tree, always partially:\n     6\t\n     7\t- **Three copies of the classifier.** v1 `type-tag`, the v2 stub's `type-tag` (which is live, since the FFI evaluates it before the no-op check), and `dao.pretty`'s `format-primitive` chain.\n     8\t- **Four independent handle detectors.** v1 telemetry, `dao.await/host-stream?`, the handoff demo's store-key convention, and the unused-for-this-purpose `dao.stream` surface predicates.\n     9\t- **Five unnamed bounds** in demos and the viewer, using take 12, 12, 40, 200 and 500, only one paired with a truncation marker.\n    10\t- **Unbounded printing into operator text** in the REPL driver, serve and connect notices, and `flush-telemetry`. `dao.pretty` accepts a depth argument on two hosts and ignores it.\n    11\t- **One correct hand-rolled instance** on Dart only: the WebSocket adapter's request summary has no JVM or Node twin.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Two findings that change the design.** v2 cursors are already plain maps with transport-namespaced keys, so v1's cursor recognition problem disappears: a cursor summarizes as ordinary data with no predicate. And every v2 handle implements the descriptor protocol, which the stream contract calls provenance rather than introspection, so a handle can be named by its identity without asking it anything. v1 also has a latent hole where sets classify as opaque.\n    14\t\n    15\t**What I proposed.** A namespace `dao.summary` with two public functions. `tag` classifies a value without entering it, and `summarize` takes explicit caller-supplied depth and item bounds and returns a uniformly tagged plain tree with true counts and a truncation flag. It requires only `dao.stream`, uses no reader conditionals, and keeps scalar dispatch for ClojureDart. Datom projection, string bounds, and extension hooks are deliberately excluded, with reasons. I recommend against an emit wrapper: `append!` is already the grep-able primitive, and a wrapper would either swallow the outcome map or be `append!` under another name. The name `dao.observe` is rejected because observe and observer already mean reading a stream in `dao.stream`.\n    16\t\n    17\t**What's next** is the owner's review, then the D2 telemetry plan as the first consumer. Nothing is staged or committed.\n    57\t     anywhere in `src/` that does informal depth/size bounding by hand\n    58\t     (a sign of the same need being solved locally, worth finding and\n    59\t     citing as evidence for the pattern's recurrence)\n    60\t   - `docs/design/*.md` — does any existing design doc already gesture\n    61\t     at this need without naming it as a shared thing?\n    62\t2. **Propose the minimal shared abstraction.** Based on what you find\n    63\t   (not preemptively from this brief's framing — verify whether the v1\n    64\t   shape is actually the right one to generalize, or whether it's\n    65\t   AST-walker-specific in ways that don't generalize cleanly). At\n    66\t   minimum, evaluate whether the abstraction is:\n    67\t   - A small, portable (CLJ/CLJS/CLJD) namespace exposing something like\n    68\t     a `type-tag`-equivalent classifier and a bounded summarizer, with\n    69\t     the depth/size bounds as explicit, caller-supplied parameters (not\n    70\t     hardcoded module constants like v1's `max-coll-items`/\n    71\t     `max-summary-depth`, since different observers may need different\n    72\t     bounds).\n    73\t   - Whether \"emit\" deserves its own tiny wrapper function at all (e.g.\n    74\t     `(when stream (append! stream value))`) for discoverability/grep-\n    75\t     ability and consistency, or whether that's trivial enough to just\n    76\t     be written inline everywhere and a wrapper would be over-\n    77\t     abstraction (weigh this against `docs/agents/malleability.md`'s\n    78\t     guidance and the project's own \"avoid cleverness\" / \"prefer simple\n    79\t     data over rich types\" code style rules).\n    80\t   - Where it should live (a new `dao.observe`-style namespace? Under\n    81\t     `dao.stream`? Somewhere else? — name it per the project's existing\n    82\t     naming conventions, check `docs/agents/vocabulary.md` for\n    83\t     precedent).\n    84\t3. **Do NOT implement VM telemetry itself** — that's separate\n    85\t   implementation work for the v1-retirement plan's D2/U3, informed by\n    86\t   whatever you propose here but not written by this task. This task's\n    87\t   deliverable is the abstraction's design and where it lives, not its\n    88\t   application to telemetry.\n    89\t\n    90\t## Deliverable\n    91\t\n    92\tWrite a short design document (a new file under `docs/design/`, name it\n    93\tper the project's conventions — something like `dao.observe.md` or\n    94\tsimilar, your call based on what you actually design) proposing the\n    95\tabstraction, citing every place you found that needs it or already\n    96\tduplicates a piece of it, and the API surface (function signatures,\n    97\tportable across CLJ/CLJS/CLJD, no reader-conditional traps — this\n    98\tproject's memory notes a specific ClojureDart `#?(:clj ...)` trap, be\n    99\tcareful if you touch reader conditionals in any example code). If you\n   100\tdetermine after real investigation that NO shared abstraction is\n   101\tactually warranted (e.g., if VM telemetry turns out to be the only real\n   102\tinstance and generalizing now would be premature abstraction against\n   103\t`docs/design/datom.world.md`'s \"Do not optimize prematurely\" /\n   104\t`CLAUDE.md`'s \"don't design for hypothetical future requirements\"), say\n   105\tso clearly and explain why, rather than inventing structure to satisfy\n   106\tthe brief. This is a design proposal, not yet reviewed or approved —\n   107\tmark it as such in the document's own status line.\n   108\t\n   109\tReport back a summary of what you found and proposed (or why you\n   110\tconcluded no abstraction is warranted). Do not implement anything\n   111\tbeyond this design document. Do not stage or commit.\n    30\t   exact served-path lookup.\"\n    31\t  [path]\n    32\t  (and (string? path)\n    33\t       (not (empty? path))\n    34\t       (str/starts-with? path \"/\")\n    35\t       (not (str/includes? path \"?\"))\n    36\t       (not (str/includes? path \"#\"))\n    37\t       (not (some #{\".\" \"..\"} (str/split path #\"/\")))))\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\t(defn descriptor?\n    41\t  \"The settled WebSocket descriptor gate.  It names a served stream, not a\n    42\t   particular connection, and contains only portable reachability data.\"\n    43\t  [x]\n    44\t  (and (map? x)\n    45\t       (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type x))\n    46\t       (string? (:dao.stream/identity x))\n    47\t       (string? (:ws/host x))\n    48\t       (integer? (:ws/port x))\n    49\t       (pos? (:ws/port x))\n    50\t       (canonical-path? (:ws/path x))\n    51\t       (transit/portable-value? x)))\n    52\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
