riptor` on that handle returns `{ok}` with both\n   205\t  `:dao.stream/descriptor`, the transport-specific reachability value, and\n   206\t  `:dao.stream/identity`, the transport-independent logical-stream identity.\n   207\t  The descriptor carries the same identity internally; the sibling projection\n   208\t  and its value inside `:dao.stream/descriptor` must be structurally equal.\n   209\t  Both projections are total and outlive any attachment, so `descriptor` has\n   210\t  no failure outcome.\n   211\t- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on an\n   212\t  attachment to the **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"**Verdict: another revision pass is needed before implementation.** The two-function abstraction is reasonable, but the current document promises boundedness, termination, and wire portability that its rules do not provide.\n\n1. **Blocking design defect — bounded sequence traversal does not guarantee bounded realization or termination.**\n\n   [dao.data.md:81](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:81) promises to realize at most `:items + 1` elements, “never more,” and calls this total because full realization is supposedly the only operation that could hang.\n\n   A lazy producer can realize a chunk when asked for one element; it can also throw, perform effects, or never produce its first element. I verified on the installed Clojure 1.12.4 runtime that taking two elements from a mapped range evaluates **32** producer elements. An expression such as `(filter constantly-false an-infinite-sequence)` cannot produce even its first element.\n\n   **Required change:** distinguish the number of elements the summarizer *requests/retains* from work performed by the producer. Either restrict accepted sequences to trusted, productive inputs and withdraw unconditional totality, or decline to realize arbitrary lazy sequences. Define exception handling separately; catching exceptions cannot solve nontermination.\n\n   The `counted?` gate itself is appropriate. However, [dao.data.md:78](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:78) incorrectly equates already-realized sequences with counted sequences. A fully realized `LazySeq` still returned `false` for `counted?` in the same runtime check. Describe the predicate’s contract, not realization status.\n\n2. **Blocking design defect — the result is not necessarily safe to ship through live v2 streams.**\n\n   [dao.data.md:55](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:55) preserves every `number?` value unchanged, while [dao.data.md:5](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:5) promises portable, safe-to-ship output.\n\n   The actual codec accepts a narrower numeric domain: JVM ratios, BigInts, BigDecimals, nonfinite numbers, and out-of-range integers are rejected by [transit.cljc:19](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/transit.cljc:19) and [transit.cljc:34](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/transit.cljc:34). Consequently, summarizing `1/3`, for example, produces a map that a WebSocket handle rejects as `:dao.stream/invalid-value`; see [ws.cljc:172](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:172).\n\n   **Required change:** define how nonportable numbers are represented or omitted. Keep classification separate from permission to preserve the original value. State that the resulting tree belongs to the actual v2 portable-value domain, and document how that guarantee works across CLJ/CLJS/CLJD. Merely avoiding reader conditionals does not establish portability.\n\n3. **Blocking design defect — `:chars` closes one size hole, but leaves other unbounded leaves.**\n\n   Strings are now bounded, correctly, at [dao.data.md:69](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:69). But keywords and symbols remain unchanged at line 55, despite potentially arbitrary name lengths. Arbitrarily large numeric representations also remain until finding 2 is resolved.\n\n   Stream identity is another bypass. Even current WebSocket identities need only satisfy `string?`; no length limit appears in [ws.cljc:40](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:40). Copying that identity directly circumvents `:chars`.\n\n   **Required change:** specify bounded representations for every variable-size leaf and for identity, or explicitly narrow the guarantee to structural bounds plus string-value bounds. The latter would not fully solve the stated unbounded-printing requirement.\n\n   The motivating citation also needs correction: [driver.cljc:384](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:384) prints only **non-string** input. A vector containing a huge string demonstrates the real problem there; a top-level string does not enter that branch. The `:chars` addition remains justified.\n\n4. **Real gap worth fixing before implementation — the stream schema confuses three distinct values.**\n\n   The table specifies `:dao.data/identity`, but [dao.data.md:90](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:90) gives `{:type :stream :identity <descriptor>}`. This both changes the key namespace and substitutes a descriptor for identity.\n\n   [stream/v2.cljc:148](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:148) distinguishes:\n\n   - The operation’s outcome map.\n   - Its `:dao.stream/descriptor` reachability value.\n   - Its `:dao.stream/identity` projection.\n\n   These are not interchangeable. Also, the conforming descriptor operation has **no failure outcome**, explicitly stated at [dao.stream.md:203](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:203).\n\n   **Required change:** name the exact extracted field, use qualified output keys consistently, and reconcile its representation with the bounds. If `:opaque` is intended for thrown exceptions or malformed third-party implementations, say so; do not imply an ordinary descriptor-failure outcome exists.\n\n5. **Real gap worth fixing before implementation — several boundary cases remain contradictory or unspecified.**\n\n   Evidence is concentrated in [dao.data.md:22](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:22), [the schema table](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:52), and [the rules](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:64).\n\n   The revision should settle these points:\n\n   - Require finite, nonnegative integer bounds and define invalid-bound behavior. Negative depth never reaches an equality check against zero.\n   - Mark collection counts and child fields as conditional in the table. Currently the table lists them unconditionally, while the rules omit them.\n   - Scope depth-zero truncation to containers. Otherwise “truncated when depth is zero” contradicts the string rule’s unconditional `false` for short strings.\n   - State explicitly that depth zero performs no sequence probe.\n   - Omit `:count` for **all** non-counted inputs, including finite sequences exhausted during probing.\n   - Specify vectors for `:items`, `:entries`, and entry pairs. This ensures eager plain output and preserves distinct set members/map entries whose summaries become identical.\n   - Define “characters”—UTF-16 code units, Unicode code points, or another unit—and the treatment of surrogate boundaries.\n\n   For maps, clarify that `:items` bounds **entries**, each containing two summarized nodes. The current “children” wording obscures that budget.\n\n6. **Real gap worth fixing before implementation — the discovery summary overstates substitutability, and the current design drops the evidence entirely.**\n\n   The original brief explicitly requires citations for every discovered site: [prompt.md:92](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1789566611000-architect-state-snapshot-abstraction.prompt.md:92). The current design contains no discovery inventory or replacement/nonreplacement assessment.\n\n   Here is the coverage audit:\n\n   | Claimed duplicate/gap | Verified evidence and assessment |\n   |---|---|\n   | Live v2 classifier | Confirmed at [telemetry.cljc:58](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/telemetry.cljc:58); evaluation before the no-op is confirmed at [ffi.cljc:209](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:209). `tag` can replace the classification responsibility, with deliberate vocabulary changes (`:host-fn` → `:fn`, handles → `:stream`, sets → `:set`). |\n   | `dao.pretty` classifier | Overstated. [pretty.cljc:49](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/pretty.cljc:49) renders scalar syntax, including string escaping; it does not produce classification data. Neither proposed function replaces that responsibility. |\n   | Ignored pretty-printer depth | Factually correct on CLJ/CLJS: [pretty.cljc:12](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/pretty.cljc:12), [pretty.cljc:22](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/pretty.cljc:22). But Dart uses depth for layout/indentation, not a truncation budget. Summarizing before printing can bound diagnostics; it does not make the printer itself a duplicate. |\n   | Await handle detection | The live analogue is [await/v2.cljc:164](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/await.cljc:164). It gates operational reader/writer handles for environment preparation. Descriptor detection could support broader recognition, but replacing it changes the predicate’s surface semantics; this is not diagnostic summarization. |\n   | Handoff store-key convention | [continuation_handoff_v2.cljc:130](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff_v2.cljc:130) identifies resources that prevent execution-state shipping. A lossy summary cannot preserve resumability or replace this policy. |\n   | Stream surface predicates | [stream/v2.cljc:194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:194) defines protocol capabilities. These remain necessary; they are dependencies of classification, not duplicates to remove. |\n   | Bounds of 12, 12, 40 | Confirmed at [compilation_pipeline_v2.cljs:454](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/datomworld/demo/compilation_pipeline_v2.cljs:454), [continuation_stream_v2.cljs:208](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream_v2.cljs:208), and [line 636](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream_v2.cljs:636). Summarization can bound their projected display data, but frame projection and first-versus-last selection remain caller policy. |\n   | Bounds of 200 and 500 | [continuation_stream_v2.cljs:644](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream_v2.cljs:644) selects recent history; [telemetry_viewer.cljs:147](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljs/yin/vm/telemetry_viewer.cljs:147) retains recent datoms. Prefix summarization does not replace either policy. The viewer is legacy evidence, not a v2 acceptance requirement. |\n   | Unbounded operator notices | Confirmed in [driver.cljc:386](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:386), [serve.cljc:348](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:348), and [connect.cljc:432](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:432). These are genuine consumers once the boundedness defects are fixed. |\n   | `flush-telemetry` | Printing exists at [repl.cljc:384](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:384), but belongs to the legacy REPL. It does not establish an additional live/v2 obligation. |\n   | Dart request summary | [dart.cljd:156](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd:156) correctly projects a host request into method/path data, but has no string-length bound. Calling it a correct instance of *bounded* summarization overstates the evidence. Generic summarization would classify the raw host request as opaque; it cannot replace the projection. |\n\n   The five numeric bounds are real, but the summary’s “only one paired with a truncation marker” is not substantiated by these expressions. A displayed original count is not an explicit truncation flag.\n\n   **Required change:** restore a compact, corrected evidence section. Keep host projection, history retention, capability checks, and resumability policy outside this abstraction; do not enlarge the API merely to absorb them.\n\n7. **Real gap worth fixing before implementation — namespace relocation conflicts with the naming guidance the brief required consulting.**\n\n   [dao.data.md:10](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.data.md:10) justifies a broad parent namespace for cross-cutting utilities. But [vocabulary.md:96](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/agents/vocabulary.md:96) explicitly advises against generic names including `data`.\n\n   Existing files do not establish the proposed utility convention: [arrays.cljc:2](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/arrays.cljc:2) describes an implementation layer for the B-tree, and [btree.cljc:19](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:19) directly requires it.\n\n   **My choice would be `dao.summary`.** It names this narrow responsibility and avoids reserving a generic parent for unrelated utilities. `dao.data` is technically legal and introduces no automatic dependency cycle; the objection is cohesion, discoverability, and inconsistency with stated guidance. If retained, acknowledge that naming exception and define its scope narrowly. Namespace ancestry alone is insufficient justification.\n\n8. **Already correct as designed — descriptor-first collection classification, ordinary cursor handling, and no emission wrapper.**\n\n   All current production v2 handle types implement the descriptor protocol: [MemoryLogHandle](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:79), [RingHandle](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:42), and [WsHandle](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:155). Checking it before collection predicates is sound. `:else :opaque` makes classification exhaustive; adding sets is appropriate. There is no demonstrated ordering defect for current handles.\n\n   Current cursors are ordinary maps: [memory_log.cljc:104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:104) and [ringbuffer.cljc:63](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:63). No cursor predicate is needed for summarization.\n\n   The document should explicitly declare `[dao.stream :as stream]`: the similarly named predicate in [dao/stream.cljc:156](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:156) detects descriptor **maps**, not v2 handles. This is dependency clarity, not a v1-compatibility requirement.\n\n   Rejecting an `emit` wrapper preserves the existing stream boundary and outcome handling. The current proposal status is also appropriate.\n\n**Before implementation:** revise the sequence guarantees, portable numeric representation, all variable-size leaves, exact stream schema, and boundary-case rules; restore the corrected evidence inventory; and resolve the namespace naming exception. The brief requires a portable design, not a completed three-host test run at this stage—but the current assertion of portability is insufficient. No files were edited."}}
